{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int/catalogue/EO:EUM:DAT:1023","copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"copyrightYear":"2025","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"dateCreated":"Wed Jan 01 00:05:00 UTC 2025","dateModified":"Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","datePublished":"Wed Jan 01 00:05:00 UTC 2025","isAccessibleForFree":"true","keywords":"Weather,Climate system monitoring - Atmosphere","license":"https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-79  77  79  -77"}},"temporalCoverage":"Wed Jan 01 00:05:00 UTC 2025 / Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","thumbnailUrl":"https://user.eumetsat.int/s3/eup-strapi-media/Fog_Low_Clouds_RGB_5e2c45fdf0.png","encodingFormat":"GeoTIFF","description":"Interpreting low clouds and fog in satellite imagery can be a challenge when using individual IR channels, due to the slight temperature difference between the cloud tops and adjacent cloud-free land. The Night Microphysics RGB solves this problem by using three different IR wavelengths.

Low water clouds and fog emit less radiation at 3.8 µm than at 10.5 µm. This brightness temperature difference (BTD) field is used in the Green Channel to highlight regions of low clouds. The channel difference in the Red channel (12.3-10.5 µm) is used as a proxy for cloud thickness and serves a similar purpose to the 10.5 µm thermal channel, enhancing areas of warm, low clouds where fog is more likely.

The Night Microphysics RGB is also an efficient tool for identifying other cloud types in the mid- and upper-atmosphere. This RGB is not designed for use during the day.","identifier":"EO:EUM:DAT:1023","name":"Fog / Low Clouds RGB - MTG - 0 degree"}

{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int/catalogue/EO:EUM:DAT:0977","copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"copyrightYear":"2017","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"dateCreated":"Sun Jan 01 00:02:00 UTC 2017","dateModified":"Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","datePublished":"Sun Jan 01 00:02:00 UTC 2017","isAccessibleForFree":"true","keywords":"MSG15,HRSEVIRI-IODC,Weather,Large-scale and synoptic systems,Atmospheric composition,Marine,Mesoscale systems and convective storms,Ocean dynamics,Cloud types","license":"https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-22  67.5  113  -67.5"}},"temporalCoverage":"Sun Jan 01 00:02:00 UTC 2017 / Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","thumbnailUrl":"https://user.eumetsat.int/s3/eup-strapi-media/L1_5_channel_7_8a6f66a75f.png","encodingFormat":"HRIT","description":"Rectified (level 1.5) Meteosat SEVIRI image data. The data is transmitted as High Rate transmissions in 12 spectral channels. L1.5 image data corresponds to the geolocated and radiometrically pre-processed image data, ready for further processing, eg, the extraction of meteorological products. Any spacecraft specific effects have been removed, and in particular, linearisation and equalisation of the image radiometry has been performed for all SEVIRI channels. The on-board blackbody data has been processed. Both radiometric and geometric quality control information is included.

Images are made available with different timeliness according to their latency: quarter-hourly images if latency is more than 3 hours and hourly images if latency is less than 3 hours (for a total of 87 images per day). To enhance the perception for areas which are on the night side of the Earth a different mapping with increased contrast is applied for IR3.9 product. The greyscale mapping is based on the EBBT which allows to map the ranges 200 K to 300 K for the night and 250 K to 330 K for the day.

From 1 June 2022, Meteosat-9 at 45.5° E is the prime satellite for the IODC service, replacing Meteosat-8 (located at 41.5° E while in operation).","identifier":"EO:EUM:DAT:0977","name":"High Rate SEVIRI Level 1.5 Image Data (1 hourly) - MSG - Indian Ocean"}

{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int/catalogue/EO:EUM:DAT:MSG:MPE-GRIB-IODC","copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"copyrightYear":"2017","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"dateCreated":"Sun Jan 01 00:02:00 UTC 2017","dateModified":"Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","datePublished":"Sun Jan 01 00:02:00 UTC 2017","isAccessibleForFree":"true","keywords":"MSGMPEG,MPE-GRIB-IODC,Weather,Severe storms,Emergency,Floods","license":"https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-22  67.5  113  -67.5"}},"temporalCoverage":"Sun Jan 01 00:02:00 UTC 2017 / Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","thumbnailUrl":"https://user.eumetsat.int/s3/eup-strapi-media/iodc_mpe_231da65824.jpg","encodingFormat":"GRIB2","description":"The Multi-Sensor Precipitation Estimate (MPE) product consists of the near-real-time rain rates in kg m-2 s-1 (kg per square meter per second) for each Meteosat image in original pixel resolution. The algorithm is based on the combination of polar orbiter microwave measurements and images in the Meteosat IR channel by a so-called blending technique. The MPE is most suitable for convective precipitation. Applications and Users: Operational weather forecasting in areas with poor or no radar coverage, especially in Africa and Asia.

The polar-orbiter microwave measurement data are crucial for the generation of the MPE product and any unavailability of the data has a direct impact on the availability of the MPE product. The polar-orbiter microwave measure data are provided by an external data provider, and any liability that may arise due to delays or interruptions in the delivery of these data and consequently of the MPE product service cannot be attributed to EUMETSAT.

From 1 June 2022, Meteosat-9 at 45.5° E is the prime satellite for the IODC service, replacing Meteosat-8 (located at 41.5° E while in operation).","identifier":"EO:EUM:DAT:MSG:MPE-GRIB-IODC","name":"Multi-Sensor Precipitation Estimate (GRIB) - MSG - Indian Ocean"}

