{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int/catalogue/EO:EUM:DAT:MSG:CON","copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"copyrightYear":"2017","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"dateCreated":"Thu Jan 12 00:07:00 UTC 2017","dateModified":"Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","datePublished":"Thu Jan 12 00:07:00 UTC 2017","isAccessibleForFree":"true","keywords":"CON,Weather,Large-scale and synoptic systems,Mesoscale systems and convective storms,Severe storms,Emergency","license":"https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-79  81  79  -81"}},"temporalCoverage":"Thu Jan 12 00:07:00 UTC 2017 / Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","thumbnailUrl":"https://user.eumetsat.int/s3/eup-strapi-media/Convection_RGB_0_degree_3cf68fa712.jpg","encodingFormat":"GeoTIFF","description":"The Convection RGB combines the brightness temperature difference (BTD) between the WV6.2 and WV7.3 channels (on red), the BTD between the IR3.9 and IR10.8 channels (on green) and the reflectance difference between the NIR1.6 and the VIS0.6 channels (on blue).

Severe convective storms appear bright yellow in this colour scheme because of the near zero BTD WV6.2-WV7.3 of overshooting Cb clouds (high red). The strong updrafts in these clouds produce small ice particles at cloud tops due to homogeneous freezing of cloud drops, resulting with large BTD IR3.9-IR10.8 (high green). Finally, large negative values of NIR1.6-VIS0.6 because of the large absorption at NIR1.6 by ice particles keeps the blue very low.

Please note that small ice crystals of Cirrus clouds should not be confused with vigorous convection. Inferred small ice crystals that are not associated with anvils of Cb clouds must form by elevated strong updrafts, such as in high altitude orographic wave clouds.","identifier":"EO:EUM:DAT:MSG:CON","name":"Convection RGB - MSG - 0 degree"}

{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int/catalogue/EO:EUM:DAT:0405","copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"copyrightYear":"2021","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"dateCreated":"Sat Jan 30 00:08:00 UTC 2021","dateModified":"Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","datePublished":"Sat Jan 30 00:08:00 UTC 2021","isAccessibleForFree":"true","keywords":"MxGAMV000200,AMVR20000,Large-scale and synoptic systems,Climate,Climate system monitoring - Atmosphere,Numerical weather prediction","license":"https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-65  65  65  -65"}},"temporalCoverage":"Sat Jan 30 00:08:00 UTC 2021 / Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","thumbnailUrl":"https://user.eumetsat.int/s3/eup-strapi-media/AMV_Compare_20050726113000_Z_MFG_20050726114500_Z_MSG_2edb40b5b3.png","encodingFormat":"netCDF4","description":"This is the second release of the reprocessed Meteosat First Generation (MFG) and Meteosat Second Generation (MSG) Atmospheric Motion Vectors (AMV) Thematic Climate Data Record (TCDR). It contains AMV at all heights below the tropopause, derived from images in 2 channels (Water Vapour 6.2, Infrared 10.8) of the instrument MVIRI on board MFG and SEVIRI on board MSG. Vectors are retrieved by tracking the motion of clouds and other atmospheric constituents such as water vapour patterns.

The height assignment of the AMVs is calculated using the Cross-Correlation Contribution (CCC) function to determine the height using the pixels that contribute the most to the vectors. The final vector is estimated averaging the speed and height over 4 consecutive images. A quality indicator is derived for each vector to assess the reliability of the retrieval.

Products are stored in netCDF4 format and generated from Meteosat-2 to Meteosat-11 satellites, covering the period from September 1981 to August 2019. This is a Thematic Climate Data Record (TCDR).","identifier":"EO:EUM:DAT:0405","name":"Atmospheric Motion Vectors Climate Data Record Release 2 - MFG and MSG - 0 degree"}

{"@context":"http://schema.org/","@type":"Dataset","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int/catalogue/EO:EUM:DAT:0590","copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"copyrightYear":"2025","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"dateCreated":"Tue Jan 14 00:03:00 UTC 2025","dateModified":"Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","datePublished":"Tue Jan 14 00:03:00 UTC 2025","isAccessibleForFree":"true","keywords":"IASxxx1C0200,Climate,Numerical weather prediction,NWP-based reanalysis","license":"https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EUMETSAT","contactPoint":{"@type":"ContactPoint","contactType":"customer support","email":"ops@eumetsat.int","url":"https://user.eumetsat.int"}},"schemaVersion":"3.5","spatialCoverage":{"@type":"Place","geo":{"@type":"GeoShape","polygon":"-180  90  180  -90"}},"temporalCoverage":"Tue Jan 14 00:03:00 UTC 2025 / Thu Jan 29 00:10:00 UTC 2026","thumbnailUrl":"https://user.eumetsat.int/s3/eup-strapi-media/iasi_91e9304add.jpg","encodingFormat":"EPS Native","description":"The main objective of the Infrared Atmospheric Sounding Interferometer (IASI) is to provide high-resolution atmospheric emission spectra to derive temperature and humidity profiles with high spectral and vertical resolution and accuracy. Additionally it is used for the determination of trace gases such as ozone, nitrous oxide, carbon dioxide and methane, as well as land and sea surface temperature, emissivity and cloud properties.

The IASI level 1c product contains infrared radiance spectra at 0.25cm-1 resolution. The product has for each sounded pixel 8461 spectral samples covering the range between 645.0 cm-1 and 2760 cm-1 wavenumbers.

This is the Metop-A and -B IASI L1c Release 2 data record. It corrects a residual non-linearity error of the order 0.1-0.2 K in the long wave part of the spectra thus improving the time and inter-instrument radiance consistencies. This release comprises L1c data from Metop-A and -B satellites for the period ranging from the 10th of July 2007 until the 30th of September 2019. This is a Fundamental Climate Data Record (FCDR), , ie, a long-term data record of calibrated and quality-controlled sensor data designed to allow the generation of homogeneous products that are accurate and stable enough for climate monitoring and data assimilation for re-analysis of the recent climate.","identifier":"EO:EUM:DAT:0590","name":"IASI Level 1C Climate Data Record Release 2 - Metop-A and -B"}

